---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/format.ts';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What OpenPlugDB is, how its data is sourced and verified, how it is licensed and how to contribute."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About OpenPlugDB</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      OpenPlugDB is an open, international and static reference of electrical plugs and connectors: EV charging
      interfaces, household plugs and sockets, industrial connectors and appliance couplers. It is two products
      built from the same sources: a versioned dataset (JSON and CSV) and this website.
    </p>

    <div class="notice" role="note">
      <strong>Important.</strong> OpenPlugDB describes connector <em>interfaces</em> as defined by standards. A
      rating is the maximum the interface is designed for. It is not the power available at a socket or charging
      station, which depends on the supply, the cable, the protection devices and the equipment. Two parts that
      fit together physically are not necessarily electrically compatible (voltage, frequency, earthing). Always
      check the rating plate of the equipment and local regulations, and have electrical work done by qualified
      people. The data is provided as is, without warranty.
    </div>

    <h2 id="model">Data model</h2>
    <p>Each connector family is one JSON file in <code>data/&lt;category&gt;/</code>. It holds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The family</strong>: stable OpenPlugDB identifier, names and aliases, category, standards with their
        editions, status and regions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Physical variants</strong>: one per role (plug, socket-outlet, vehicle inlet, appliance inlet…),
        with the gender of its contacts, the contact list, keying, mechanical notes and what it mates with. The role
        and the contact gender are separate: a CEE 7/7 plug has male pins and a female earth hole.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Electrical profiles</strong>: AC or DC, supply, phases, neutral and earth, voltage and current with
        their qualifier (rated, maximum or range), frequency, and the function of each contact. A family can have
        several profiles, e.g. AC single-phase, AC three-phase and DC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drawings</strong>: SVG files with their kind, orientation, author, license and provenance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mappings</strong> to OCPI 2.2.1 and 2.3.0, Open Charge Map and OpenStreetMap, checked against
        vocabularies kept in <code>mappings/</code>.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Sources</strong> with access dates, a confidence level per profile and a verification status.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In the JSON, <code>null</code> means unknown (not documented in the cited sources), while a missing key means
      not applicable (for example no frequency on a DC profile).
    </p>

    <h2 id="methodology">Methodology</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Values come from standards (official abstracts and preview samples), specifications and manufacturer
        datasheets. Encyclopedic sources are only used as secondary sources, and flagged as such.
      </li>
      <li>No electrical value is derived or estimated. A maximum power is only recorded when a source states it.</li>
      <li>
        Product ratings are not connector ratings: when a manufacturer value is cited, it is only an example.
      </li>
      <li>
        Families whose data still has gaps are marked <span class="badge partial">partial</span>, with the gaps
        listed on their page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every change is checked automatically: JSON Schema, cross references, external mappings, SVG structure and
        electrical consistency (phases, contact functions, voltage reference, power bound).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="drawings">Drawings</h2>
    <p>
      Front views show the mating face, seen straight on, as you look at the part before connecting it. The
      opposite gender is drawn separately and never obtained by mirroring. Filled circles are pins (male contacts),
      rings are contact tubes (female contacts). Drawings are schematic: relative contact positions follow the
      cited sources, dimensions are not to scale. A drawing is only published once its layout is confirmed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="licenses">Licenses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Code</strong> (site, scripts, schemas): MIT.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Data</strong> (<code>data/</code>, <code>mappings/</code>, exports): CC0-1.0. The data consists of
        facts compiled by the project. External identifiers (OCPI, Open Charge Map, OpenStreetMap) are listed for
        interoperability only; their specification texts are not reproduced.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drawings</strong>: each file has its own license, shown next to it. Original OpenPlugDB drawings are
        CC-BY-4.0.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Icons</strong>: interface icons from <a href="https://github.com/evicons/evicons">EVicons</a> are MIT
        (Copyright (c) 2026 Landolsi Digital) and are shipped unchanged with their license file; icons OpenPlugDB
        draws in the same style for connectors EVicons does not cover are MIT too (OpenPlugDB contributors); each
        icon shows its own license next to it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Details: <a href="https://github.com/ovarilo/openplugdb/blob/main/docs/licensing.md">licensing policy</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/ovarilo/openplugdb/blob/main/docs/provenance.md">provenance policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contributing">Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Corrections and new connectors are welcome on
      <a href="https://github.com/ovarilo/openplugdb">GitHub</a>. Each connector page has a “Suggest a correction”
      link to its source file. Please cite a source for every value: see
      <a href="https://github.com/ovarilo/openplugdb/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href={url('/connectors/')}>Browse the catalog →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .notice {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
